  "2026-09-08T05:47:23.848Z","harness:codex","Duplicate check: complete all-status 2658-item title/metadata catalog and source ledger reviewed; current query submodular token selection nearest pm-4k6b,pm-5t33or,pm-t2t709,pm-doxj,pm-bfa1ob. No individual owner for this experiment was found. Shared scorer/signal/packer baselines are prerequisites, not proof of this distinct mechanism. User requested individual planning records; this draft preserves the open cognitive-architecture decision gate and does not accept the ADR. Estimate is a one-day initial spike budget, not a delivery forecast."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-09-08T06:11:47.320Z","harness:codex","Mathematical correction to the historical proposal: plain marginal-value-per-token greedy is a heuristic, not automatically a 1-1/e knapsack approximation. Sviridenko (2004) obtains that bound for a nonnegative nondecreasing submodular objective using partial enumeration plus greedy completion; https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167637703000622 . First prove or refute monotonicity and diminishing returns for the actual objective. Negative redundancy/risk penalties and exclusive representation levels can invalidate the assumptions. On tiny candidate sets compute an exhaustive optimum and measure the heuristic gap; at scale cap candidate generation and report empirical quality/cost rather than borrowing an inapplicable theorem."
body: "## Proposal and provenance\n\nStable algorithm slug: `submodular-token-selection`. Source: pm-4k6b note recorded 2026-07-07T15:12:12.161Z.\n\n(4) Token-budget packing as submodular selection: greedy by marginal-value-per-token (MMR-style redundancy penalty so two near-duplicate items do not both spend budget), with per-item projection degradation levels (full body -> summary -> id+title) as fractional knapsack steps. Greedy is provably near-optimal for submodular objectives and is deterministic.\nAll four stay SDK-side behind scoreContextCandidates; none require new stored state beyond what history streams and the read index already hold.\n\n## Delivery boundary\n\nEvaluate this mechanism as a bounded public-SDK experiment before any production adoption. Use the existing scorer and packer contracts; no CLI-only behavior or new command noun. The historical catalog is design input, not evidence that this mechanism was implemented.\n\n## Experiment protocol\n\nFreeze a workspace/history revision and parameter manifest. Compare against the current deterministic SDK baseline using held-out judgments and negative controls. Report nDCG/action success, critical-evidence omission, latency p50/p95, peak memory, tokens per completed task, and rebuild cost separately. Include 3-item/minutes, real-shaped, 100k/1M-item, decades-long, and sparse/dense/imbalanced graph scenarios; smoke subsets may not claim scale acceptance. Bound candidate counts, graph expansions, elapsed time, and output size, with cancellation and completeness receipts. Record exact versus approximate behavior and approximation error; theoretical guarantees require their stated assumptions. Missing or untrusted evidence remains unknown, never inferred proof.\n\nPromotion requires a reproducible quality improvement on the held-out target population, no regression of existing exact invariants or mandatory gates, and a declared compute/token cost justified by that improvement. Reject or defer with the same immutable evidence if those conditions fail. No production default, adaptive learning, or data export is authorized by this planning item."
